  "thinking_process": "The previous attempt failed with a `SEGFAULT RISK` on `flat_order_ptr`. The root cause was allocating a struct on the stack using `alloc_struct` inside a loop and then passing its pointer to an external `append_func`. The `append_func` presumably stores this pointer. When the loop iterates, the stack frame is destroyed, leaving a dangling pointer in the external list, which leads to a segfault upon access. The mistake was assuming `alloc_struct` performs heap allocation; it performs stack allocation, making the memory temporary. To fix this, I will fundamentally change the interaction with the `append_func`. Instead of creating a struct locally and passing a pointer to it, I will pass all the individual data fields (`order_id`, `user_id`, `is_vip`, etc.) as separate arguments to the `append_func` FFI call. This new design delegates the responsibility of memory allocation and data storage entirely to the `append_func` implementation. It is now assumed that `append_func` will receive the values, allocate persistent (heap) memory for them, and store them correctly. This completely avoids creating temporary stack pointers that are stored beyond their lifetime, thus eliminating the dangling pointer and segfault risk.",
